Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

PG0 found nine of the brief's twenty-one canonical events missing, and the
last five structurally unreachable: appointment, estimate, acceptance,
deposit and job_complete are all SERVER writes, and lib/analytics.ts
`track()` returns early when `window` is undefined. No amount of client
work reaches them. This is the other half of the measurement foundation.

WHY NOT GA4'S MEASUREMENT PROTOCOL

It was the obvious answer and it was rejected for three reasons, written
into the schema so nobody rediscovers them:

  1. Measurement Protocol requires a `client_id`, which identifies a
     DEVICE. Nothing on the server has one. Synthesising it from the design
     id would file every deposit under a fabricated user and inflate GA4's
     user counts with people who do not exist. A number wrong in a
     flattering direction is the worst kind.
  2. These are commercial facts about transactions — a deposit cleared, a
     job finished — not observations of a person's behaviour. They belong
     in the business's own records, not a third party's.
  3. GA4 could not answer the success question anyway. It needs revenue and
     cost, and those live in Postgres.

So GA4 keeps the top of the funnel, where consent and device identity are
real and correct. This table takes the commercial end. MEAS-01's design id
spans both.

SIX STAGES, SIX CALL SITES

  LEAD_CAPTURED       api/leads after the QuoteRequest lands
  APPOINTMENT_BOOKED  api/appointments, OUTSIDE the transaction
  QUOTE_ISSUED        sendEstimateEmail — a price in the customer's hands
  QUOTE_ACCEPTED      convertQuoteToProject
  DEPOSIT_PAID        the Stripe webhook, DEPOSIT invoices only
  JOB_COMPLETED       updateProjectStatus on COMPLETED

None is awaited. A deposit that cleared is a deposit that cleared whether
or not its ledger row lands, and `await` in the Stripe handler would turn a
failed analytics insert into a non-2xx, making Stripe retry a webhook
against an invoice already marked PAID. The existing `already paid` guard
is what makes the deposit row idempotent across those retries.

DEPOSIT_PAID is guarded to `invoice.stage === 'DEPOSIT'`. Counting progress
and final invoices would report a conversion rate above 100% for every job
that invoices in thirds.

JOB_COMPLETED fires from the status change, not from JobOutcome, because
JobOutcome is filled in by an estimator at their own pace and may be days
late or never written.

THE READ PATH SHIPS IN THE SAME PATCH

PG0 classified FloorAssessment, AssessmentPhoto and FrameworkScoring as
WRITE-ONLY — captured correctly, consented correctly, never read by any
code. A table nobody reads is indistinguishable from a table nobody
writes, except that it costs more and looks like progress. So /admin/funnel
ships here, linked from the admin chrome, and it answers the PG0 success
question on its face: of the last 90 days' deposits, how many began in
Floor Studio.

It refuses to show a margin. Margin needs actual labour and material cost
against a prediction, which is ECON-01; a margin built from quoted figures
would look like evidence and would not be. It also refuses to print 0% for
an empty stage — zero leads is the absence of a conversion rate, not a
business that is failing, and somebody acts on the difference.

WHAT THE GUARDS DID NOT CATCH

`amountCad: projectData.contractValue ?? Number(...) || null` is a
SyntaxError — `??` cannot be mixed with `||` unbracketed. parse-scan
reported zero diagnostics on it. I found it by reading the line back, fixed
it with explicit parentheses, and then ran every changed file through a
syntax-only tsc pass. That pass is how I know the rest is clean, and it is
worth noting that the existing guard would have shipped it.

PRIVACY

The table holds a stage, a timestamp, an amount and bare indexed UUIDs. No
name, no email, no phone, no address, no IP. Not a shadow CRM — the
customer record already exists on QuoteRequest and User, and duplicating it
is what Rule #5 forbids. A test asserts the schema block contains none of
those fields, so it stays true.

Verified: 65 of 66 guards (verify:css needs postcss from node_modules,
absent in this sandbox; re-run in full before the push). Migrations guard
clean — 27 models, 18 enums, 9 migrations, no drift. parse-scan 605 files,
zero diagnostics. 55 new assertions, including six that read the
instrumented files and assert the call sites still exist, because
instrumentation is the code most likely to be deleted by accident and its
absence is silent.

